<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1360 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Personent hodie</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin Christmas song for children's voices, more than 600 years old, first printed in a Finnish school songbook in 1582.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (a highly similar melody is in a manuscript of 1360 from Moosburg)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the cathedral song schools of medieval Europe; later Finnish and Swedish schools and English choirs</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Moosburg, Bavaria, Germany (the earliest manuscript); printed for Finland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>usually dated from a manuscript of 1360; first printed 1582</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous medieval Latin words, printed in Piae Cantiones, 1582; a similar melody in a manuscript of 1360)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/personent-hodie">Personent hodie</a>&quot; is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> first published in 1582 in Piae Cantiones, a Finnish song book of 74 medieval songs with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts, collected by Jacobus Finno (Jaakko Suomalainen), a Lutheran cleric, and published by T. P. Rutha. The book grew out of the libraries of cathedral song schools, whose repertory had strong links with medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where clerical students from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had studied for generations. A highly similar melody is found in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1360 from the Bavarian city of Moosburg, and the song is usually dated from that manuscript.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text is probably a parody, in the musical sense, of a 12th-century song beginning &quot;Intonent hodie voces ecclesie&quot;, written in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, patron saint of sailors and children, whose feast is 6 December. Hugh Keyte and Andrew Parrott point out that two of the verses have an unusual double repeat of a syllable. In the older song these repeats illustrated the three boys and the three girls whom Saint Nicholas saved, the boys from drowning and the girls from prostitution. The text was probably rewritten for the Feast of the Holy Innocents, 28 December, when the choristers and their &quot;boy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>&quot; traditionally took the places of the senior clergy in the choir stalls. The carol is still often linked with Holy Innocents' Day.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> from Piae Cantiones went on being sung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> until the 19th century. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the book became known after a rare copy, once owned by Peter of Nyland, was given to the British Minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, who gave it to John Mason Neale in 1852. From that copy Neale and Thomas Helmore published songs in two collections, in 1853 and 1854. Neale's &quot;Let the Song be Begun&quot; uses the melody but not the text of the carol.</p><p>The carol became more prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after Gustav Holst arranged it for unison voices and orchestra in 1916. With the accompaniment reduced for organ, his version is often used as a processional hymn in churches and cathedrals and often forms part of the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols; it was sung in the service broadcast from King's College Chapel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 2011. Holst's setting is sometimes called &quot;Theodoric&quot;, after his middle name, Theodore, and under that name it is the tune of Percy Dearmer's hymn &quot;God is love, his the care&quot;. The most common English version of the words, &quot;On this day earth shall ring&quot;, is by Jane M. Joseph (1894–1929), who published it under the name &quot;James M. Joseph&quot;. Elizabeth Poston made another, the &quot;Boys' Carol&quot;, in 1965, and the tune was also used for the hymn &quot;Long Ago, Prophets Knew&quot;.</p><p>Later arrangements include a harmonised choral version by Hugh Keyte and Andrew Parrott in the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols, one by Antony Pitts in the Naxos Book of Carols, two by John Rutter and a Festival Procession by Lara Hoggard with brass, orchestra and organ. Maddy Prior and The Carnival Band recorded it with period instruments on A Tapestry of Carols (1987), and the quartet Anonymous 4 recorded the song with its older &quot;Intonent hodie&quot; text on Legends of St. Nicholas. The jazz musician Wayne Shorter recorded the melody as &quot;12th Century Carol&quot; in 2003.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call for children's voices to ring out today in praise of the child born for us. The verses tell of his birth into the world, of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a> who came with gifts, and end by calling on clergy and choirboys alike to sing like the angels, with glory to God in the highest. The mood is loud, bright and processional.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Personent hodie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let them resound today,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">voces puerulae,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the voices of children,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">laudantes iucunde</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>joyfully praising</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">qui nobis est natus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>him who is born for us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">summo Deo datus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>given by God most high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et de vir, vir, vir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of a vir-, vir-, vir-,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et de vir, vir</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of a vir-, vir-,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et de virgineo ventre procreatus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and brought forth from a virgin's womb.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">In mundo nascitur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He is born into the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">pannis involvitur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he is wrapped in swaddling cloths,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">praesepi ponitur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he is laid in a manger</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">stabulo brutorum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in a stable of beasts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">rector supernorum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the ruler of the heavens.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Perdidit, dit, dit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He has lost, lost, lost,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">perdidit, dit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he has lost, lost,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">perdidit spolia princeps infernorum.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the prince of hell has lost his spoils.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Magi tres venerunt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Three Magi came,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">munera offerunt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they offer gifts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">parvulum inquirunt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they seek the little child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">stellulam sequendo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>following the little star,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ipsum adorando,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>worshipping him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">aurum, thus, thus, thus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gold, incense, incense, incense,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">aurum thus, thus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gold, incense, incense,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">aurum, thus, et myrrham ei offerendo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>offering him gold, incense and myrrh.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Omnes clericuli,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let all the young clerks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">pariter pueri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the boys alike</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">cantent ut angeli:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>sing as the angels do:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">advenisti mundo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you have come to the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">laudes tibi fundo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I pour out praises to you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ideo, o, o,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Therefore, O, O,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ideo, o</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>therefore, O,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ideo gloria in excelsis Deo.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>therefore glory to God in the highest.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous; the Latin text of Piae Cantiones (1582), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personent_hodie" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Personent hodie</a>); public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>Lines 6 and 7 of each stanza repeat a syllable of the word that opens line 8 (vir, vir of virgineo; dit, dit of perdidit; thus; o of ideo), as the tune asks; the translation repeats a word or syllable in the same places.</li><li>Line 1.2: voces puerulae, literally 'boyish voices', the voices of the choirboys.</li><li>Line 1.4: the Latin has no 'him': 'praising [the one] who is born for us'.</li><li>Line 3.6: thus, frankincense.</li><li>Line 4.1: clericuli, 'little clerics': the young clerks of a cathedral or school choir.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
